# Flow Dependencies

A Flow is a dependency graph. A node becomes ready when its predecessor nodes
have completed. The graph comes from result references and explicit `after:`
options. The authoring `group` and `branch` forms do not add runtime ordering.

Run the code cells from top to bottom.

## Install Jido Action

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_action, "~> 3.0"}])
```

## Define An Echo Action

```elixir
defmodule FlowDependencies.Echo do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "flow_dependencies_echo",
    schema: Zoi.map(),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl true
  def run(params, _context), do: {:ok, params}
end
```

## Build A Diamond With Implicit And Explicit Edges

The `:implicit` step reads `:source`, so the result reference creates an edge.
The `:explicit` step reads only Flow input, but `after: :source` still creates
an ordering edge.

```elixir
defmodule FlowDependencies.Diamond do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_dependencies_diamond",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    source_result = step(:source, FlowDependencies.Echo, with: %{value: input(:value)})

    implicit_result =
      step(:implicit, FlowDependencies.Echo,
        with: %{value: select(source_result, :value)}
      )

    explicit_result =
      step(:explicit, FlowDependencies.Echo,
        with: %{value: input(:value)},
        after: :source
      )

    group do
      branch :left_branch do
        left_result = step(:left, FlowDependencies.Echo, with: %{value: input(:value)})
      end

      branch :right_branch do
        right_result = step(:right, FlowDependencies.Echo, with: %{value: input(:value)})
      end
    end

    joined_result =
      step(:joined, FlowDependencies.Echo,
        with: %{
          implicit: select(implicit_result, :value),
          explicit: select(explicit_result, :value),
          left: select(left_result, :value),
          right: select(right_result, :value)
        }
      )

    return(joined_result)
  end
end
```

Inspect the direct predecessors. The group branch names are not dependencies;
the `left` and `right` steps are independent roots.

```elixir
flow = FlowDependencies.Diamond.flow()

{:ok, dependencies} = Jido.Flow.dependencies(flow)

["source"] = dependencies["implicit"]
["source"] = dependencies["explicit"]
["explicit", "implicit", "left", "right"] = dependencies["joined"]
[] = dependencies["left"]
[] = dependencies["right"]
```

## Ready Waves Are Canonical

Start a paused execution to inspect the graph at runtime. `ready/1` returns
ready node names in canonical order. `wave/1` executes the current ready set;
nodes that become ready wait for the next wave.

```elixir
{:ok, execution} = Jido.Exec.start(FlowDependencies.Diamond, %{value: 2}, %{})

["left", "right", "source"] = Jido.Exec.ready(execution)

{:ok, first_wave, execution} = Jido.Exec.wave(execution)
true = Enum.sort(Enum.map(first_wave, & &1.node)) == ["left", "right", "source"]

["explicit", "implicit"] = Jido.Exec.ready(execution)

{:ok, _second_wave, execution} = Jido.Exec.wave(execution)
["joined"] = Jido.Exec.ready(execution)

{:ok, execution} = Jido.Exec.continue(execution)
{:ok, %{implicit: 2, explicit: 2, left: 2, right: 2}} = Jido.Exec.result(execution)
```

The first wave has three independent roots. The join waits for all four
predecessors, even though `left` and `right` came from a group.

## Groups Do Not Turn On Parallel Execution

`group` and `branch` are authoring and provenance forms. They do not turn on
parallel execution. There is no public `parallel` DSL primitive.

Enable concurrent scheduling at the `Jido.Exec` boundary with `async: true` and
`max_concurrency`:

```elixir
{:ok, %{implicit: 2, explicit: 2, left: 2, right: 2}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(
    FlowDependencies.Diamond,
    %{value: 2},
    %{},
    async: true,
    max_concurrency: 2
  )
```

The option applies to ready waves. The graph still decides which nodes are
independent and which nodes must wait.

